# Cybersecurity Lab 1 - DVWA, Docker and Burp Suite

## Goal
Set up a local vulnerable web application lab and understand how Burp Suite intercepts HTTP traffic between the browser and the server.

## Tools Used
- Kali Linux
- Docker
- DVWA
- Burp Suite Community Edition
- Burp Browser

## What I Practiced
- Pulling a Docker image
- Running DVWA inside a Docker container
- Mapping host and container ports
- Accessing DVWA through localhost
- Configuring Burp Proxy
- Intercepting HTTP traffic
- Understanding GET and POST requests
- Finding form data inside the request body
- Modifying a login request before forwarding it to the server

## Key Commands
```bash
docker pull vulnerables/web-dvwa
docker run --rm -it -p 8080:80 vulnerables/web-dvwa
docker ps

Port Mapping
-p 8080:80
This means:
Kali host port 8080 -> DVWA container port 80
DVWA was available at:
http://127.0.0.1:8080
Burp Proxy was configured on:
127.0.0.1:8081
HTTP Requests Observed
Initial page request:
GET / HTTP/1.1
Host: 127.0.0.1:8080
Login request:
POST /login.php HTTP/1.1
Host: 127.0.0.1:8080
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded
Cookie: PHPSESSID=...; security=low
Request body:
username=admin&password=password&Login=Login&user_token=...
What I Learned
GET is used to request data from the server.
POST is used to send data to the server.
HTTP request goes from the browser/client to the server.
HTTP response goes from the server to the browser/client.
Content-Type describes the format of the data.
The actual form data is stored in the request body.
Burp Suite can intercept and modify a request before it reaches the server.
Practical Test
I changed this value in Burp Suite:
username=admin
to:
username=administrator
After forwarding the modified request, DVWA returned:
Login failed
Conclusion
This lab confirmed that Burp Suite can intercept, inspect, and modify HTTP requests between the browser and the web server in a controlled local environment.
Next Step
Learn Burp Suite HTTP History and Repeater to analyze and resend captured requests more efficiently.
